@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1276 = private unnamed_addr constant [731 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for a shebang directive in `.py` files that does not contain `python`,\0A`pytest`, or `uv run`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, a shebang (also known as a hashbang) is the first line of a\0Ascript, which specifies the command that should be used to run the\0Ascript.\0A\0AFor Python scripts, if the shebang does not include a command that explicitly\0Aor implicitly specifies an interpreter, then the file will be executed with\0Athe default interpreter, which is likely a mistake.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0A#!/usr/bin/env bash\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A#!/usr/bin/env python3\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Executable Python Scripts](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/appendix.html#executable-python-scripts)\0A", align 1
