@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1582 = private unnamed_addr constant [836 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary string literal or float casts in `Decimal`\0Aconstructors.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `Decimal` constructor accepts a variety of arguments, including\0Aintegers, floats, and strings. However, it's not necessary to cast\0Ainteger literals to strings when passing them to the `Decimal`.\0A\0ASimilarly, `Decimal` accepts `inf`, `-inf`, and `nan` as string literals,\0Aso there's no need to wrap those values in a `float` call when passing\0Athem to the `Decimal` constructor.\0A\0APrefer the more concise form of argument passing for `Decimal`\0Aconstructors, as it's more readable and idiomatic.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0ADecimal(\220\22)\0ADecimal(float(\22Infinity\22))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0ADecimal(0)\0ADecimal(\22Infinity\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `decimal`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/decimal.html)\0A", align 1
