@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1586 = private unnamed_addr constant [1305 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the use of implicit `Optional` in type annotations when the\0Adefault parameter value is `None`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AImplicit `Optional` is prohibited by [PEP 484]. It is confusing and\0Ainconsistent with the rest of the type system.\0A\0AIt's recommended to use `Optional[T]` instead. For Python 3.10 and later,\0Ayou can also use `T | None`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef foo(arg: int = None):\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom typing import Optional\0A\0A\0Adef foo(arg: Optional[int] = None):\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AOr, for Python 3.10 and later:\0A```python\0Adef foo(arg: int | None = None):\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AIf you want to use the `|` operator in Python 3.9 and earlier, you can\0Ause future imports:\0A```python\0Afrom __future__ import annotations\0A\0A\0Adef foo(arg: int | None = None):\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0A## Limitations\0A\0AType aliases are not supported and could result in false negatives.\0AFor example, the following code will not be flagged:\0A```python\0AText = str | bytes\0A\0A\0Adef foo(arg: Text = None):\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis fix is always marked as unsafe because it can change the behavior of code that relies on\0Atype hints, and it assumes the default value is always appropriate\E2\80\94which might not be the case.\0A\0A[PEP 484]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/#union-types\0A", align 1
